# 000700-000799/20170502T203846Z_778389557.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=6bb|+Conceptually, line%2Doriented is the same as character%2Doriented, except that line%2Doriented has an open%2Dended comparison alphabet (every possible line—of arbitrary length—is treated as a unique symbol), while character%2Doriented has a closed comparison alphabet, which is often small in practice (e.g. the 8%2Dbit [[ASCII]] printable subset used in most source code). %0A%0AHistorically, diff assumes that lines are a string of unibyte characters. Many features will still work to some degree with [[UTF%2D8]], because the line%2Dend character in UTF%2D8 is transparent (every '\n' byte in UTF%2D8 represents newline).  Unless the diff implementation provides explicit [[Unicode]] support, [[UTF%2D16]] or [[UTF%2D32]] won't work properly at all.  Among the problems involved when handling UTF%2D8 as a unibyte character sequence, a whitespace%2Dinsensitive comparison mode unaware of Unicode will fail to recognize many additional Unicode [[whitespace character]] codepoints as legitimate whitespace. %0A%0A|=68f9|
