# 000700-000799/20170819T070051Z_796205302.wiki
zΔ⚡b|=6be|+History %3D%3D%0A%0AThe <tt>diff</tt> utility was developed in the early 1970s on the Unix operating system which was emerging from [[Bell Labs]] in Murray Hill, New Jersey. The final version, first shipped with the 5th Edition of Unix in 1974, was entirely written by [[Douglas McIlroy]].  This research was published in a 1976 paper co%2Dwritten with James W. Hunt who developed an initial prototype of <tt>diff</tt>.<ref name%3D"diff paper">{{cite journal%7Cauthor1%3DJames W. Hunt %7Cauthor2%3DM. Douglas McIlroy %7Ctitle%3DAn Algorithm for Differential File Comparison%7Cvolume%3D41%7Cjournal%3DComputing Science Technical Report, Bell Laboratories%7Cdate%3DJune 1976%7Cpages%3D%7Curl%3Dhttp://www.cs.dartmouth.edu/~doug/diff.pdf}}</ref>  The algorithm this paper described became known as the [[Hunt–McIlroy algorithm]].%0A%0AMcIlroy's work was preceded and influenced by [[Stephen C. Johnson%7CSteve Johnson]]'s comparison program on [[GECOS]] and [[Mike Lesk]]'s <tt>proof</tt> program.  <tt>Proof</tt> also originated on Unix and, like <tt>diff</tt>, produced line%2Dby%2Dline changes and even used angle%2Dbrackets (">" and "&lt;") for presenting line insertions and deletions in the program's output.  The [[heuristic]]s used in these early applications were, however, deemed unreliable.  The potential usefulness of a diff tool provoked McIlroy into researching and designing a more robust tool that could be used in a variety of tasks but perform well in the processing and size limitations of the [[PDP%2D11]]'s hardware.  His approach to the problem resulted from collaboration also with individuals at Bell Labs including [[Alfred Aho]], Elliot Pinson, [[Jeffrey Ullman]], and Harold S. Stone.%0A%0AIn the context of Unix, the use of the <tt>[[ed (Unix)%7Ced]]</tt> line editor provided <tt>diff</tt> with the natural ability to create machine%2Dusable "edit scripts".  These edit scripts, when saved to a file, can, along with the original file, be reconstituted by <tt>ed</tt> into the modified file in its entirety.  This greatly reduced the [[secondary storage]] necessary to maintain multiple versions of a file.  McIlroy considered writing a post%2Dprocessor for <tt>diff</tt> where a variety of output formats could be designed and implemented, but he found it more frugal and simpler to have <tt>diff</tt> be responsible for generating the syntax and reverse%2Dorder input accepted by the <tt>ed</tt> command.%0A%0ALate in 1984 [[Larry Wall]] created a separate utility, [[patch (Unix)%7Cpatch]],%0Areleasing its source code on the ''mod.sources'' and ''net.sources'' newsgroups.<ref>{{cite newsgroup%0A %7C title %3D A patch applier%2D%2DYOU WANT THIS!!!%0A %7C author %3D Larry Wall%0A %7C date %3D November 9, 1984%0A %7C newsgroup %3D net.sources%0A %7C message%2Did %3D 1457@sdcrdcf.UUCP%0A %7C url %3D https://groups.google.com/d/msg/net.sources/qtfVio1sSHs/G0cPT5HFDFcJ%0A %7C access%2Ddate %3D May 11, 2015%0A }}</ref><ref>{{cite newsgroup%0A %7C title %3D patch version 1.2%2D%2DYOU WANT THIS%0A %7C author %3D Larry Wall%0A %7C date %3D November 29, 1984%0A %7C newsgroup %3D net.sources%0A %7C message%2Did %3D 1508@sdcrdcf.UUCP%0A %7C url %3D https://groups.google.com/d/msg/net.sources/uWFr9NOp_fw/SRS_P2vSgFgJ%0A %7C access%2Ddate %3D May 11, 2015%0A }}</ref><ref>{{cite newsgroup%0A %7C title %3D patch version 1.3%0A %7C author %3D Larry Wall%0A %7C date %3D May 8, 1985%0A %7C newsgroup %3D net.sources%0A %7C message%2Did %3D 813@genrad.UUCP%0A %7C url %3D https://groups.google.com/d/msg/mod.sources/xSQM63e39YY/apNNJSkJi0gJ%0A %7C access%2Ddate %3D May 11, 2015%0A }}</ref>%0AThis program generalized and extended the ability to modify files with output from <tt>diff</tt>.%0A%0AModes in [[Emacs]] also allow for converting the format of patches and even editing patches interactively.%0A%0AIn <tt>diff</tt>'s early years, common uses included comparing changes in the source of software code and markup for technical documents, verifying program debugging output, comparing filesystem listings and analyzing computer assembly code.  The output targeted for <tt>ed</tt> was motivated to provide compression for a sequence of modifications made to a file.  The [[Source Code Control System]] (SCCS) and its ability to archive revisions emerged in the late 1970s as a consequence of storing edit scripts from <tt>diff</tt>.%0A%0A%3D%3D |=502a|+Free file comparison tools%3D%3D%0A{{colbegin}}%0A*[[cmp (Unix)%7Ccmp]]%0A*[[comm]]%0A*[[Diff%2DText]]%0A*[[diff3]]%0A*[[Kompare]]%0A*[[tkdiff]]%0A*[[WinMerge]] (Microsoft Windows)%0A*[[meld (software)%7Cmeld]]%0A*[[Pretty Diff]]%0A*[[spiff (UNIX)%7Cspiff]]%0A{{colend}}%0A%0A%3D%3D|=159|+%0A%0A%3D%3DNotes%3D%3D%0A{{reflist%7C30em}}%0A*{{cite journal%7Cauthor%3DPaul Heckel%7Ctitle%3DA technique for isolating differences between files%7Cjournal%3DCommunications of the ACM%7Cvolume%3D21%7Cissue%3D4%7Cdate%3DApril 1978%7Cpages%3D264&#x2013;268%7Cdoi%3D10.1145/359460.359467}}[http://doi.acm.org/10.1145/359460.359467]%0A*A generic implementation of the Myers SES/LCS algorithm with the Hirschberg linear space refinement [http://www.ioplex.com/~miallen/libmba/dl/src/diff.c (C source code)]|=4a3|
